{"candidates":[{"content":"Artifact 18174034ea0c contradicts 2f8f85328fae on where the determinism obligation for append-only-log replay resides: 18174034ea0c assigns it to the writer (idempotency ensures the log is identical regardless of retry count, because a safe-but-non-idempotent write produces duplicates that are 'fatal to deterministic replay'), whereas 2f8f85328fae assigns it to the consumer's fold operation (the log merely stores facts; correctness under at-least-once delivery depends on the fold being commutative and duplicate-aware, so the writer's only obligation is to append). The contradiction is genuine because each framework declares the other's locus insufficient: 18174034ea0c calls writer-level safety 'necessary but not sufficient,' while 2f8f85328fae calls writer-level idempotency unnecessary. This relation is refuted if the two obligations are complementary rather than contradictory—i.e., if writer idempotency for log-level determinism and fold commutativity for consumer-level tolerance are jointly and independently required, so that each artifact addresses a distinct layer without precluding the other.","evidence_refs":[{"block":"18174034ea0c22d562ec","quote":"A safe but non-idempotent write could produce a log with duplicates, which is harmless in isolation but fatal to deterministic replay."},{"block":"2f8f85328fae446c","quote":"the event log itself is merely an ordered sequence of facts; correctness under at-least-once delivery depends on the consumer's fold operation being commutative"},{"block":"3fc33b3603be","quote":"An idempotent operation satisfies f(f(x))=f(x): repeated application yields the same result as a single application."}],"refs":[{"role":"dependence","target":"fe91ea9f2df4d0678d6bb472a0cbb4efb03d324dfabaa8010614ec783870b8c9"},{"role":"dependence","target":"2f8f85328fae446c3c422bd4286bfcd4df6abfd26babf3005820e939993c4d0b"},{"role":"dependence","target":"3fc33b3603be4326107111e73ee5bfa735e6f92a6da6a64286fc895870602e42"}],"typic